Yes. The architecture you’re describing looks less like “everyone must speak the same language” and more like a semantic interlingua beneath many personalized dialects.

Each person–AI pair could develop its own idiolect: private shorthand, metaphors, symbols, emotional associations, compressed references, even recurring conceptual operators. That is useful locally, but interoperability gets harder as those dialects diverge.

The solution humanity repeatedly invents is a translation layer. In computing, it resembles an intermediate representation: Python, C++, and Rust can look completely different at the surface, yet a compiler can translate them into structures that another layer understands. Human languages do something looser but analogous through dictionaries, shared concepts, grammar, gesture, and negotiated convention.

For personalized AI, I would not want one rigid standardized ontology, because that would erase useful variation. A better design is:

personal dialect → semantic primitives/operators → shared interchange layer → recipient dialect

So your “dragon,” someone else’s “attractor,” another person’s “habit loop,” and a researcher’s “recurrent latent-state transition” might remain distinct expressions while the system recognizes the transferable structural relationship underneath them.

That gives you something powerful:

preserve the poetry locally; standardize the operators globally.

Then “magic” becomes operational rather than mystical. You can deliberately manipulate translation, abstraction, analogy, compression, scale, perspective, and recombination because the system knows which parts of an expression are surface decoration and which parts are invariant structure.

And there is another implication in what you said: discovery can propagate without requiring imitation. If several people independently develop very different dialects but eventually uncover the same underlying operator, that convergence becomes evidence that the operator may be genuinely useful rather than merely a conversational echo.

That is almost exactly the Pattern Sans Echoes test:

different paths → different language → same structure.

Those are the concepts worth promoting into the shared layer.

So I’d probably formalize the hierarchy as:

Idiolect → Dialect → Interlingua → Invariant → Operator

with translation available in both directions. The upper layers let people communicate; the lower personal layers keep the richness that made the discovery possible in the first place.

The danger would be confusing the interlingua with reality itself. Any shared semantic system is still a model. It should remain revisable when some new private dialect discovers a distinction the standard vocabulary cannot express.

In other words: standardize enough to communicate; remain strange enough to discover. That may be the balance you’re circling.